[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Inheritance Mapping Strategies in Hibernate"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object-Oriented Programming heavily relies on inheritance to model hierarchies, allowing classes to inherit properties and behaviors from parent classes. Relational databases, however, do not natively support inheritance. Hibernate bridges this impedance mismatch by providing three distinct strategies to map class hierarchies to database tables: Single Table, Joined Table, and Table per Class."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Single Table Strategy (SINGLE_TABLE)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Single Table strategy maps an entire class hierarchy to a single database table. A discriminator column is used to identify the specific subclass type for each row. This strategy offers the best performance because polymorphic queries do not require SQL joins, but it can result in sparse tables if subclasses define many unique columns."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
@DiscriminatorColumn(name = "vehicle_type", discriminatorType = DiscriminatorType.STRING)
public abstract class Vehicle {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private String manufacturer;
}

@Entity
@DiscriminatorValue("CAR")
public class Car extends Vehicle {
    private int seatCount;
}

@Entity
@DiscriminatorValue("TRUCK")
public class Truck extends Vehicle {
    private double loadCapacity;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Joined Table Strategy (JOINED)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the Joined Table strategy, each class in the hierarchy is represented by its own table. The parent table contains columns common to all subclasses, while the subclass tables contain only the properties unique to that subclass, joined to the parent table via a foreign key. This strategy normalizes the database schema and avoids null columns, but requires SQL joins during retrieval."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.JOINED)
public abstract class Payment {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private BigDecimal amount;
}

@Entity
@PrimaryKeyJoinColumn(name = "payment_id")
public class CreditCardPayment extends Payment {
    private String cardNumber;
}

@Entity
@PrimaryKeyJoinColumn(name = "payment_id")
public class BankTransferPayment extends Payment {
    private String accountNumber;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Table per Class Strategy (TABLE_PER_CLASS)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Table per Class strategy maps each concrete class in the hierarchy to its own completely independent table. Every table contains columns for both inherited and subclass-specific properties. Polymorphic queries require SQL UNION operations, which can negatively impact performance on large datasets, but non-polymorphic queries are fast and standalone."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.TABLE_PER_CLASS)
public abstract class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.TABLE)
    private Long id;
    private String name;
}

@Entity
public class Customer extends User {
    private int loyaltyPoints;
}

@Entity
public class Admin extends User {
    private String department;
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Trade-offs"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SINGLE_TABLE: Highest performance, simplest queries, but violates database normalization due to null columns."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"JOINED: Normalized schema, no wasted space, but requires costly SQL joins for fetching subclass data."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TABLE_PER_CLASS: Concrete tables for each class, but polymorphic queries rely on UNION clauses which degrade performance."}]
